hadiths[186]{hadithnumber,text}:
"17455","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever meets Allah, Mighty and Majestic, associating nothing with Him, pays the zakah of his wealth with a willing soul seeking reward, and listens and obeys, then Paradise is for him — or he said, he shall enter Paradise — while five sins have no expiation: associating partners with Allah, killing a soul unjustly, slandering a believer, fleeing from the battlefield, or a binding oath by which one wrongfully seizes wealth. > **""Whoever meets Allah associating nothing with Him, pays his zakah willingly seeking reward, and listens and obeys, then Paradise is for him; and five have no expiation: shirk, unjust killing, slandering a believer, fleeing from battle, or a binding oath seizing wealth unjustly.""**"
"17456","ʿUmar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to ʿA'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, concerning those who divided their religion and became sects, that they are the people of innovation and desires, they have no repentance, and he is free of them and they are free of him. > **""O ʿA'ishah, those who divided their religion and became sects are the people of innovation and desires; they have no repentance. I am free of them and they are free of me.""**"
"17457","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah has veiled repentance from every adherent of innovation. > **""Allah has veiled repentance from every adherent of innovation.""**"
"17458","ʿA'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that those who commit foul deeds are destroyed, meaning those who commit filthy sins. > **""Destroyed are those who commit foul sins.""**"
"17459","ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to beware of despised minor sins, for they gather against a man until they destroy him, and he struck the example of people camping in a desert who each bring a stick until they gather a pile, kindle a fire, and cook what they placed therein. > **""Beware of despised minor sins, for they gather against a man until they destroy him, like travellers who each bring a stick until they kindle a fire and cook their food.""**"
"17460","ʿAbdullah — meaning Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him — reported from the Prophet ﷺ that Satan has despaired of idols being worshipped in Arabia, but he will settle for despised minor sins which destroy on the Day of Resurrection, so avoid injustices as much as possible, for a servant will come with good deeds thinking they will save him, yet claimants will say his Lord wronged them, and his good deeds will be erased until none remain, like travellers without firewood who scatter, gather wood, kindle a great fire, and cook; so are sins. > **""Satan has despaired of idols being worshipped in Arabia, but he will settle for despised sins which destroy on the Day of Resurrection; avoid injustices as much as you can.""**"
"17461","Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the example of despised sins is like travellers halting in a barren land with food that needs fire, so they scatter and one brings dung, another a bone, another a stick, until they gather enough to prepare their food; likewise the committer of minor sins tells a lie here and commits a sin there, gathering what may throw him face-first into Hell. > **""The committer of minor sins gathers lie upon sin until they cast him face-first into Hell, like travellers gathering fuel until they can cook their food.""**"
"17462","Sahl ibn Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to beware of despised sins, for their example is like people halting in a valley, each bringing a stick until they carry enough to bake their bread; and when their owner is seized for them, they destroy him. > **""Beware of despised sins, for they are like sticks gathered one by one until bread can be baked; when their owner is seized for them, they destroy him.""**"
"17463","Saʿd ibn Junadah, may Allah be pleased with him, said that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished Hunayn they camped on barren land with nothing in it, so the Prophet ﷺ said to gather whatever each found, even a bone or fragment, and within an hour they made a heap, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ said, Do you see this? So sins gather against a man just as you gathered this; so let a man fear Allah and commit neither minor nor major sin, for they are recorded against him. > **""Do you see this heap? So sins gather against a man; let him fear Allah and commit neither minor nor major sin, for they are recorded against him.""**"
"17464","Abu Saʿid — meaning al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him — said that people do deeds finer in their eyes than a hair, which they used to count among the destructive sins in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""You do deeds finer in your eyes than a hair, which we counted among the destructive sins in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"17465","ʿUbadah ibn Qarad — or Qaraz, may Allah be pleased with him — said that people today do deeds finer in their eyes than a hair, which they counted among the destructive sins in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, whereupon Humayd asked Abu Qatadah what he would say if he saw their time, and he replied he would say it even more emphatically. > **""You do deeds finer than a hair in your eyes, which we counted among the destructive sins in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"17466","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that a woman was punished for a cat — or she-cat — which she tied until it died, never releasing it to eat from the vermin of the earth, so Hell became due for her thereby. > **""A woman was punished for a cat she tied until it died, never releasing it to eat from the vermin of the earth, so Hell became due for her.""**"
"17467","ʿAlqamah reported that they were with ʿA'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, when Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, entered, and she asked if he was the one who narrates that a woman was punished for a cat she tied, neither feeding nor watering it, and he said he heard it from the Prophet ﷺ, so she asked whether he knew that woman was a disbeliever besides her deed, for a believer is too honourable to Allah to be punished for a cat, and told him to consider how he narrates from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""A believer is too honourable to Allah to be punished for a cat; consider how you narrate from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"17468","Abu al-Darda', may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that if what they do to animals were forgiven them, much else would be forgiven them. > **""If what you do to animals were forgiven you, much else would be forgiven you.""**"
"17469","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAs, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said from the minbar to show mercy so mercy is shown, to forgive so forgiveness is granted, and woe to the funnels of speech and woe to the persistent who persist in what they did knowingly. > **""Show mercy and you will be shown mercy; forgive and you will be forgiven; woe to the persistent who persist in what they did knowingly.""**"
"17470","ʿAbdullah ibn Mughaffal, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a man met a woman who had been immoral in Jahiliyyah and began flirting until he stretched his hand to her, but she said to stop, for Allah has removed polytheism and Jahiliyyah and brought Islam, so the man turned away, struck his face on a wall and cut it, then came to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him, whereupon he said he was a servant for whom Allah intended good, for when Allah intends good for a servant He hastens the punishment of his sin, and when He intends evil He withholds it until He recompenses him on the Day of Resurrection like Mount ʿAyr. > **""You are a servant for whom Allah intended good; when Allah intends good for a servant He hastens his punishment in this world, and when He intends evil He withholds it until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17471","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ with blood flowing from his face and said he had followed a woman with his gaze until a wall struck him, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when Allah intends good for His servant He hastens the punishment of his sin in this world, and when He intends evil He withholds it until the Day of Resurrection, when it will be like Mount ʿAyr. > **""When Allah intends good for His servant He hastens his punishment in this world, and when He intends evil He withholds his sin until the Day of Resurrection, when it will be like Mount ʿAyr.""**"
"17472","ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a woman passed by a man who fixed his gaze upon her, then he passed by a wall which scraped his face, so he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with blood flowing and confessed what he had done, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when Allah intends good for a servant He hastens his punishment in this world, but when He intends otherwise He grants respite for his sins until they are fully recompensed on the Day of Resurrection, like Mount ʿAyr. > **""When Allah intends good for a servant He hastens his punishment in this world, and when He intends otherwise He grants him respite until the Day of Resurrection, when it will be like Mount ʿAyr.""**"
"17473","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that a prophet complained to his Lord that a believing, obedient servant is denied the world and exposed to trials, while a disbelieving, disobedient servant is spared trials and given the world, so Allah revealed to him that all servants and lands belong to Him and all things glorify Him, and that the believing servant has sins for which his Lord withholds the world and sends trials so he comes with only good deeds to reward, while the disbelieving servant has good deeds for which his Lord withholds trials and spreads the world before him so he comes with only evil deeds to repay. > **""As for My believing servant who has sins, I withhold the world from him and send him trials until he comes to Me to be rewarded for his good deeds; and as for My disbelieving servant who has good deeds, I withhold trials from him and spread the world before him until he comes to Me to be repaid for his evil deeds.""**"
"17474","ʿA'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when a servant's sins multiply and he has nothing to expiate them, Allah afflicts him with grief in order to expiate them from him. > **""When a servant's sins multiply and he has nothing to expiate them, Allah afflicts him with grief to expiate them from him.""**"
"17475","Abu Qatadah al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that all his ummah will be pardoned except the open proclaimers, and when asked who they are, he said it is one who does a deed by night which his Lord covers, then in the morning says to someone that he did such-and-such last night, thereby uncovering Allah's covering from himself. > **""All my ummah will be pardoned except the open proclaimers: one who does a deed by night which his Lord covers, then in the morning says, O so-and-so, I did such-and-such last night, thereby uncovering Allah's covering from himself.""**"
"17476","Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that no sin which Allah covers for a servant in this world will He use to disgrace him on the Day of Resurrection. > **""No sin which Allah covers for a servant in this world will He use to disgrace him on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17477","ʿAlqamah al-Muzani reported from his father that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that no sin which Allah covers for a servant in this world but Allah will cover it for him in the Hereafter. > **""No sin which Allah covers for a servant in this world but Allah will cover it for him in the Hereafter.""**"
"17478","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever does not show mercy will not be shown mercy, whoever does not forgive will not be forgiven, and whoever does not repent will not be turned to. > **""Whoever does not show mercy will not be shown mercy; whoever does not forgive will not be forgiven; and whoever does not repent will not be turned to.""**"
"17479","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever does not show mercy will not be shown mercy. > **""Whoever does not show mercy will not be shown mercy.""**"
"17480","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to show leniency and leniency will be shown to you. > **""Show leniency and leniency will be shown to you.""**"
"17481","Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, conveying from Allah, not to place His knowing, monotheist, sinful servants in Paradise or Hell until He Himself places them according to His knowledge of them, nor to take upon themselves what was not imposed, nor to judge servants instead of their Lord, Mighty and Majestic. > **""Do not place My knowing, monotheist, sinful servants in Paradise or Hell until I place them according to My knowledge; do not take on what was not imposed on you, and do not judge servants instead of their Lord.""**"
"17482","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that when decisive verses were revealed, such as those concerning devouring orphans' wealth unjustly, devouring usury, and intentionally killing a believer whose recompense is Hell, they used to testify that whoever did any of these was in the Fire, but when Allah revealed that He does not forgive shirk but forgives less than that for whom He wills, they refrained from testifying and both hoped and feared for them because of what Allah had made binding. > **""We used to testify that whoever did these deeds was in the Fire, until Allah revealed that He does not forgive shirk but forgives less than that for whom He wills, so we refrained and hoped for them and feared for them.""**"
"17483","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that they used to say of the killer of a believer who died, and of one who committed a major sin and died upon it, that he was in the Fire, until the verse that Allah does not forgive shirk but forgives less than that for whom He wills was revealed, after which they no longer declared it binding but hoped for them and feared for them. > **""We used to say the killer and the major sinner who died upon it were in the Fire, until Allah revealed that He forgives less than shirk for whom He wills, so we hoped for them and feared for them.""**"
"17484","ʿAbdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that they used to declare the people of major sins bound for Hell until Allah revealed that He does not forgive shirk but forgives less than that for whom He wills, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade them to declare any of the monotheists bound for the Fire. > **""We used to declare the people of major sins bound for Hell until this verse was revealed, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade us to declare any monotheist bound for the Fire.""**"
"17485","ʿAbdullah — meaning Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him — reported from the Prophet ﷺ that a man was praying when another came and stepped on his neck, so the one beneath said that by Allah he would never be forgiven, whereupon Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said that His servant had sworn upon Him that He would not forgive His servant, yet He had forgiven him. > **""My servant swore upon Me that I would not forgive My servant, yet I have forgiven him.""**"
"17486","ʿAbdullah — meaning Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him — said not to rush to praise people or blame them, for one may see something pleasing in his brother today that displeases him tomorrow, and vice versa, for people are deceived; Allah forgives sins on the Day of Resurrection, and Allah is more merciful to His servant on the day he meets Him than a mother who spreads a bed for her child in the shade then feels it first, so that any thorn or sting strikes her before him. > **""Do not rush to praise or blame people; Allah is more merciful to His servant on the day he meets Him than a mother who feels the bed lest any thorn strike her child before her.""**"
"17487","ʿAbdullah — meaning Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him — reported that a man who had never done any good at all except tawhid, when death came to him, told his family to take him, burn him until he became coal, grind him, and scatter him in the sea on a windy day, which they did, and he found himself in the grasp of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, Who asked what carried him to do what he did, and he replied fear of Him, so Allah forgave him. > **""What carried you to do what you did? Fear of You. Then Allah forgave him.""**"
"17488","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ the like of the preceding narration about the man who instructed his family to burn and scatter him. > **""Like the preceding narration of the man who feared Allah and instructed his family to burn and scatter him.""**"
"17489","ʿAbdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a man of great wealth, when death came to him, told his family that if they did as he commanded he would bequeath them much wealth, and they agreed, so he commanded that when he died they burn him, grind him, and on a windy day climb a mountaintop and scatter him, for if Allah seized him He would not forgive him; this was done to him, he was gathered into the hand of Allah Who asked what carried him to do it, and he replied fear of his Lord, so He said to go, for He had forgiven him. > **""O my Lord, fear of You. Go, for I have forgiven you.""**"
"17490","In one narration, the man was a grave-robber, and he was forgiven on account of his fear of Allah. > **""He was a grave-robber, and he was forgiven on account of his fear.""**"
"17491","ʿAbdullah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the man fell into the hand of Allah, Who asked what carried him to do what he did, and he replied fear of Him, so He said He had forgiven him. > **""What carried you to do what you did? Fear of You. I have forgiven you.""**"
"17492","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ, and likewise Ibn Sirin reported from the Prophet ﷺ, that a man among those before them had never done any good at all except tawhid, and when dying told his family to burn him until coal, grind him, and scatter him on a windy day, which they did upon his death, and he found himself in the grasp of Allah, Who said, O son of Adam, what carried you to do what you did? He replied, O my Lord, fear of You, so He forgave him thereby, though he had done no good at all except tawhid. > **""O son of Adam, what carried you to do what you did? O my Lord, fear of You. Then He forgave him for it.""**"
"17493","Muʿawiyah ibn Haydah, may Allah be pleased with him, heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that a servant of Allah whom Allah had given wealth and children, yet who kept no religion for Allah, lived until his life was nearly gone, realised he had stored no good with Allah, called his sons and asked what kind of father they knew him to be, and they said the best father, so he swore he would take back every wealth of his with them unless they did as he commanded, took their covenant, and commanded that when he died they cast him into fire until coal, then grind and scatter him in the wind so he might escape Allah; this was done to him after his death, he was brought forth in the best form and presented to his Lord, Who asked what carried him to the fire, and he replied fear of his Lord, so his repentance was accepted. > **""What carried you to the fire? Fear of You, O my Lord. Then his repentance was accepted.""**"
"17494","In one narration, the Lord said, O son of Adam, what carried you to do what you did? He replied, out of fear of You, so Allah, Mighty and Majestic, met him with forgiveness thereby. > **""O son of Adam, what carried you to do what you did? Out of fear of You. Then Allah met him with forgiveness.""**"
"17495","Salman — meaning al-Farisi, may Allah be pleased with him — reported from the Prophet ﷺ, like the preceding narration, that a man among those before them whom Allah had enriched with wealth and children told his family that when he died they should burn him, and when he became coal grind and scatter him, for if his Lord seized him He would punish him as none in the worlds; they did so, Allah commanded and he was gathered standing before Allah, Who asked what carried him to do it, and he replied fear of his Lord, so he was forgiven. > **""What carried you to do what you did? Fear of You, O my Lord. Then he was forgiven.""**"
"17496","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that Quraysh said to the Prophet ﷺ to ask his Lord to make Safa into gold for them, promising to follow him if it became gold, so he supplicated his Lord, Jibril, peace be upon him, came and said his Lord sent greetings and offered that if he wished Safa would become gold but whoever disbelieved thereafter would be punished as none in the worlds, or if he wished the door of repentance and mercy would be opened for them, and he chose the door of repentance and mercy. > **""Rather, the door of repentance and mercy.""**"
"17497","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that for years in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ they recited the verse concerning those who do not invoke another god with Allah, nor kill the soul Allah forbade except by right, nor commit adultery, until the verse except whoever repents and believes was revealed, and he never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rejoice more intensely than at it, and at the verse indeed We granted you a clear victory. > **""Except whoever repents and believes. I never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rejoice more than at this verse and at: Indeed We granted you a clear victory.""**"
"17498","Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah is happier with the repentance of His servant who wronged himself than a traveller in a deadly desert who loses his mount in the middle, searches right and left until exhausted — or despairing — thinking himself doomed, then finds it in a place he never hoped, so Allah is happier with the repentance of His excessive servant than that man with his mount when he finds it. > **""Allah is happier with the repentance of His servant who wronged himself than a traveller in a deadly desert who loses his mount, despairs of life, then finds it where he never hoped.""**"
"17499","Abu Saʿid, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever draws near to Allah a span, He draws near to him a cubit, whoever draws near a cubit, He draws near a fathom, and whoever comes to Him walking, He comes to him running. > **""Whoever draws near to Allah a span, He draws near to him a cubit; whoever draws near a cubit, He draws near a fathom; and whoever comes to Him walking, He comes to him running.""**"
"17500","Shurayh reported that he heard a man from among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ say that the Prophet ﷺ said Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says, O son of Adam, stand to Me and I walk to you, and walk to Me and I hasten to you. > **""O son of Adam, stand to Me and I walk to you; walk to Me and I hasten to you.""**"
"17501","Yazid ibn Nuʿaym reported that he heard Abu Dharr al-Ghifari, may Allah be pleased with him, upon the minbar at Fustat say that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say that whoever draws near to Allah a span, He draws near a cubit, whoever draws near a cubit, He draws near a fathom, and whoever comes to Allah walking, Allah comes to him hastening, and Allah is Most High and Most Great, repeated thrice. > **""Whoever draws near to Allah a span, He draws near to him a cubit; whoever draws near a cubit, He draws near a fathom; and whoever comes to Allah walking, Allah comes to him hastening, and Allah is Most High and Most Great.""**"
"17502","Salman — raising it as marfuʿ — reported that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says that when His servant draws near to Him a span He draws near a cubit, when he draws near a cubit He draws near a fathom, and when he comes walking He comes hastening. > **""When My servant draws near to Me a span I draw near to him a cubit; when he draws near to Me a cubit I draw near to him a fathom; and when he comes to Me walking I come to him hastening.""**"
"17503","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that whoever repents a year before his death, repentance is accepted from him, and whoever repents a month before his death, repentance is accepted, continuing until he mentioned a day, then an hour, then the briefest moment, whereupon a man asked what if he were a disbeliever who then embraced Islam, and he replied that he was only narrating as he heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""Whoever repents a year before his death, repentance is accepted from him; and whoever repents a month before his death, repentance is accepted — until even a day, an hour, the briefest moment before death.""**"
"17504","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that whoever repents before his death by the time of milking a camel, Allah turns to him. > **""Whoever repents before his death by the time of milking a camel, Allah turns to him.""**"
"17505","ʿAbd al-Rahman al-Baylamani reported that four of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gathered, and one said he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that Allah accepts His servant's repentance a day before he dies, the second asked if he truly heard it and affirmed, adding that he heard Him say half a day before death, the third likewise added the forenoon before death, and the fourth said he heard Him say that Allah accepts His servant's repentance so long as he has not reached the death rattle. > **""Allah accepts His servant's repentance a day before he dies; half a day before; in the forenoon before; so long as he has not reached the death rattle.""**"
"17506","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that no believing servant repents to Allah a month before death but Allah accepts it, or even less, a day or an hour before death, if Allah knows repentance and sincerity in him, but Allah accepts it from him. > **""No believing servant repents to Allah a month before death, or a day or an hour before, with Allah knowing repentance and sincerity in him, but Allah accepts it from him.""**"
"17507","ʿAbdullah ibn Salam, may Allah be pleased with him, said he would narrate only from a sent prophet or a revealed Book: that if a servant committed every sin then repented to Allah a day before his death, it would be accepted from him. > **""If a servant committed every sin then repented to Allah a day before his death, it would be accepted from him.""**"
"17508","ʿAbdullah ibn Salam, may Allah be pleased with him, said he would narrate only from a revealed Book or a sent prophet: that no soul repents before the illness in which it dies but its repentance is accepted, until the sun rises from the west. > **""No soul repents before the illness in which it dies but its repentance is accepted, until the sun rises from the west.""**"
"17509","ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Paradise has eight gates, seven closed and one gate open for repentance until the sun rises from its direction. > **""Paradise has eight gates, seven closed and one gate open for repentance until the sun rises from its direction.""**"
"17510","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that whoever repents before the sun rises from the west, Allah turns to him. > **""Whoever repents before the sun rises from the west, Allah turns to him.""**"
"17511","ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, said that repentance is offered to the son of Adam if he accepts it, so long as one of three has not emerged: the sun rising from the west, the Beast emerging, or Gog and Magog emerging. > **""Repentance is offered to the son of Adam so long as the sun has not risen from the west, the Beast has not emerged, and Gog and Magog have not emerged.""**"
"17512","Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah accepts His servant's repentance — or forgives His servant — so long as the veil has not fallen, and when asked what the falling of the veil is, he said the soul departing while associating partners with Allah. > **""Allah accepts His servant's repentance so long as the veil has not fallen: the soul departing while associating partners with Allah.""**"
"17513","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah, Blessed and Exalted, continues to accept repentance from His servant so long as he has not reached the death rattle. > **""Allah continues to accept repentance from His servant so long as he has not reached the death rattle.""**"
"17514","ʿA'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to her, O ʿA'ishah, if you have committed a sin then seek Allah's forgiveness, for repentance from sin is remorse and seeking forgiveness. > **""O ʿA'ishah, if you have committed a sin then seek Allah's forgiveness, for repentance from sin is remorse and seeking forgiveness.""**"
"17515","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the expiation of sin is remorse. > **""The expiation of sin is remorse.""**"
"17516","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the remorseful one awaits repentance, while the self-admiring one awaits wrath. > **""The remorseful one awaits repentance, while the self-admiring one awaits wrath.""**"
"17517","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that remorse is repentance. > **""Remorse is repentance.""**"
"17518","Wa'il ibn Hujr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that remorse is repentance. > **""Remorse is repentance.""**"
"17519","The son of Abu Saʿid reported from his father that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that remorse is repentance, and the one who repents from sin is like one with no sin. > **""Remorse is repentance, and the one who repents from sin is like one with no sin.""**"
"17520","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that remorse is repentance. > **""Remorse is repentance.""**"
"17521","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that a servant commits a sin, then when he remembers it what he did grieves him, and when Allah sees him grieved by what he did, He forgives him. > **""A servant commits a sin, then what he did grieves him when he remembers it, and when Allah sees him grieved by what he did, He forgives him.""**"
"17522","ʿA'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever commits a sin then feels remorse, Allah forgives him that sin before he even seeks forgiveness, and whoever receives a blessing from Allah and knows it is from Allah, Allah records gratitude for him before he praises Him for it, and whoever Allah clothes with a garment and knows Allah clothed him, he is forgiven before the garment reaches his knees. > **""Whoever commits a sin then feels remorse, Allah forgives him before he seeks forgiveness; whoever knows a blessing is from Allah, gratitude is recorded before he praises; and whoever knows Allah clothed him is forgiven before the garment reaches his knees.""**"
"17523","al-Aswad ibn Sariʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a captive was brought to the Prophet ﷺ and said, O Allah, I repent to You and I do not repent to Muhammad, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ said that he recognised the right for its people. > **""He recognised the right for its people.""**"
"17524","ʿAbdullah — meaning Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him — reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that repentance from sin is to repent from it, then not return to it. > **""Repentance from sin is to repent from it, then not return to it.""**"
"17525","ʿAwf ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, said there is no sin but he knows its repentance, and when asked what its repentance is, he said to abandon it then never return to it. > **""There is no sin but I know its repentance: to abandon it then never return to it.""**"
"17526","ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the one who repents from sin is like one with no sin. > **""The one who repents from sin is like one with no sin.""**"
"17527","The son of Abu Saʿid reported from his father that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that remorse is repentance, and the one who repents from sin is like one with no sin. > **""Remorse is repentance, and the one who repents from sin is like one with no sin.""**"
"17528","ʿA'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever wishes to outstrip the constant, striving worshipper should refrain from sins. > **""Whoever wishes to outstrip the constant, striving worshipper should refrain from sins.""**"
"17529","ʿAli ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, loves the believing servant who is repeatedly tested yet constantly repentant. > **""Allah loves the believing servant who is repeatedly tested yet constantly repentant.""**"
"17530","ʿUqbah ibn ʿAmir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said that one of them commits a sin, and he said it is recorded against him; then he seeks forgiveness and repents, and he said he is forgiven and turned to; then he returns and sins again, and he said it is recorded against him; then he seeks forgiveness and repents, and he said he is forgiven and turned to, and Allah never tires until you tire. > **""It is recorded against him; then he seeks forgiveness and repents, so he is forgiven and turned to; and Allah never tires until you tire.""**"
"17531","ʿA'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that Habib ibn al-Harith came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said he was a man given to sins, so he told him to repent to Allah, O Habib; he said he repents then returns, so he told him that whenever he sins he should repent; he said then his sins would multiply, so he said that Allah's pardon is greater than his sins, O Habib ibn al-Harith. > **""Whenever you sin, repent; Allah's pardon is greater than your sins, O Habib ibn al-Harith.""**"
"17532","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said he had sinned, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him that when he sins he should seek his Lord's forgiveness; he said he seeks forgiveness then returns to sin, so he told him that when he sins he should return and seek forgiveness; this was repeated until the fourth time, when he said that when he sins he should seek his Lord's forgiveness until Satan is the loser. > **""When you sin, seek your Lord's forgiveness, until Satan is the loser.""**"
"17533","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that no believing servant is without a sin he returns to time after time, or a sin he remains upon without parting from it until he parts from life; yet the believer was created tested, oft-repenting, forgetful — but when reminded, he remembers. > **""The believer was created tested, oft-repenting, forgetful — but when reminded, he remembers.""**"
"17534","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the example of the believer and faith is like a horse on its tether: it roams then returns to its tether; the believer slips then returns to faith; so feed the God-fearing with your food and bestow your kindness upon the believers. > **""The believer slips then returns to faith, like a horse that roams then returns to its tether; so feed the God-fearing and bestow kindness upon the believers.""**"
"17535","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the believer is frail yet mending — sinning then repenting and seeking forgiveness — so blessed is he who perishes upon his mending. > **""The believer is frail yet mending; blessed is he who perishes upon his mending.""**"
"17536","ʿUqbah ibn ʿAmir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the example of one who does evil deeds then does good deeds is like a man wearing a tight coat of mail that chokes him, then each good deed loosens a ring, then another, until it falls to the ground. > **""One who does evil then good is like a man in a choking coat of mail, each good deed loosening a ring until it falls to the ground.""**"
"17537","Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever does good in what remains of life, what passed is forgiven him, but whoever does evil in what remains is seized for what passed and what remains. > **""Whoever does good in what remains is forgiven what passed; but whoever does evil in what remains is seized for what passed and what remains.""**"
"17538","Abu Tawil Shatb al-Mamdud, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked about one who committed every sin, leaving none, small or great, without committing it — is there repentance for him? He asked whether he had embraced Islam, and he testified that none is worthy of worship but Allah and that he is Allah's Messenger, so he told him to do good deeds and abandon evil deeds, and Allah would turn them all into good for him; he asked, even my betrayals and enormities? He said yes, whereupon the man cried Allah is Greatest and kept crying it until he disappeared. > **""Do good deeds and abandon evil deeds, and Allah will turn them all into good for you — even your betrayals and enormities.""**"
"17539","Thawban, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that when a servant persistently seeks Allah's pleasure, Allah says to Jibril that so-and-so seeks to please Him and His mercy is upon him, so Jibril proclaims mercy upon him, then the bearers of the Throne and those around them, until the inhabitants of the seven heavens proclaim it, then it descends to earth. > **""So-and-so seeks to please Me, and My mercy is upon him — proclaimed by Jibril, the bearers of the Throne, the seven heavens, then earth.""**"
"17540","ʿAmr ibn Malik al-Ru'asi, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he came to the Prophet ﷺ who turned away from him, so he said that the Lord, Blessed and Exalted, accepts excuses and is pleased, so be pleased with him, and he was pleased with him. > **""The Lord accepts excuses and is pleased, so be pleased with me — and he was pleased with him.""**"
"17541","Umm al-Fadl, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Prophet ﷺ visited al-ʿAbbas while he was ill and wishing for death, so he said, O ʿAbbas, uncle of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, do not wish for death: if you are a doer of good, increasing good upon your good is better for you, and if you are a doer of evil, being granted respite to seek pardon is better for you; do not wish for death. > **""O ʿAbbas, do not wish for death: if you are good, more good is better for you; if you are evil, respite to seek pardon is better for you.""**"
"17542","In one narration, the Prophet ﷺ said that if he is a doer of evil, being delayed so he may seek pardon for his evil is better for him. > **""If you are a doer of evil, being delayed so you may seek pardon for your evil is better for you.""**"
"17543","Jabir ibn ʿAbdillah, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said not to wish for death, for the terror of what lies ahead is severe, and part of felicity is that a servant's life be long and Allah grant him turning back to Him. > **""Do not wish for death, for the terror ahead is severe; part of felicity is long life with Allah granting repentance.""**"
"17544","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that they sat with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who exhorted them until Saʿd wept profusely and wished he were dead, so the Prophet ﷺ said, O Saʿd, do you wish for death in my presence? — repeating it thrice — then said, O Saʿd, if you were created for Paradise, then the longer your life and the better your deeds, the better for you. > **""O Saʿd, if you were created for Paradise, the longer your life and the better your deeds, the better for you.""**"
"17545","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether he should inform them of the best among them, and they said yes, so he said the best among them are the longest-lived with the best deeds. > **""Shall I inform you of the best among you? The longest-lived among you with the best deeds.""**"
"17546","In one narration, the Prophet ﷺ said the best in manners, in place of the best in deeds. > **""The best among you are the longest-lived with the best manners.""**"
"17547","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether he should inform them of the best, and they said yes, so he said the best are the longest-lived, when they remain upright. > **""The best among you are the longest-lived, when they remain upright.""**"
"17548","Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a man asked which people are best, and the Prophet ﷺ said one whose life is long and deeds are good; he asked which people are worst, and he said one whose life is long and deeds are evil. > **""The best of people is one whose life is long and deeds are good; the worst is one whose life is long and deeds are evil.""**"
"17549","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether he should inform them of the best, and they said yes, so he said the best are the finest in manners and the longest in years. > **""The best among you are the finest in manners and the longest in years.""**"
"17550","ʿUbadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he asked whether he should inform them of the best, and they said yes, so he said the longest-lived in Islam, when they remain upright. > **""The best among you are the longest-lived in Islam, when they remain upright.""**"
"17551","ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah has servants whom He spares from perishing, prolongs their lives in good deeds, grants them wholesome provision, keeps them alive in well-being, takes their souls in well-being upon their beds, and grants them the stations of martyrs. > **""Allah has servants whom He spares from perishing, prolongs their lives in good deeds, grants them wholesome provision, keeps them in well-being, takes them in well-being, and grants them the stations of martyrs.""**"
"17552","Shaddad Abu ʿAmmar reported that ʿAwf ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, said, O plague, take me to you, whereupon they said, did you not hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that the longer a Muslim's life, the better for him? He replied, yes indeed. > **""Did you not hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that the longer a Muslim's life, the better for him? Yes indeed.""**"
"17553","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that two men of Bali, a clan of Qudaʿah, embraced Islam with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; one was martyred and the other lived a year longer, and Talhah ibn ʿUbaydillah, may Allah be pleased with him, saw in a dream that the latter entered Paradise before the martyr, marvelled at that, and mentioned it to the Prophet ﷺ in the morning, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, did he not fast Ramadan after him, pray six thousand rakʿahs and so many voluntary prayers? > **""Did he not fast Ramadan after him, pray six thousand rakʿahs and so many voluntary prayers?""**"
"17554","ʿAbdullah ibn Shaddad reported that three men of Banu ʿUdhrah came to the Prophet ﷺ and embraced Islam, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked who would host them, so Talhah, may Allah be pleased with him, took them in; the Prophet ﷺ sent an expedition in which one went and was martyred, then another expedition in which the second was martyred, then the third died upon his bed; Talhah saw the three in Paradise in a dream, with the one who died upon his bed foremost, then the later martyr, then the earliest martyr last, which perplexed him, so he mentioned it to the Prophet ﷺ, who said, what do you find strange? None is more excellent with Allah than a believer granted long life in Islam for his glorification, exaltation, and declaration of unity. > **""What do you find strange? None is more excellent with Allah than a believer granted long life in Islam for his glorification, exaltation, and declaration of unity.""**"
"17555","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, raising it as marfuʿ, reported that the child until puberty has his good deeds recorded for his parent — or parents — while his evil is recorded against neither him nor his parents; when he reaches puberty the Pen runs and the two angels with him are commanded to guard strictly; when he reaches forty in Islam Allah secures him from three afflictions — madness, leprosy, and vitiligo; at fifty Allah lightens his reckoning; at sixty Allah grants him turning to Him in what He loves; at seventy the inhabitants of heaven love him; at eighty Allah records his good and overlooks his evil; at ninety Allah forgives his past and future sins, lets him intercede for his household, and he becomes Allah's captive on earth; and at extreme old age, when he knows nothing after knowledge, Allah records for him the like of the good he did in health, while no evil is recorded against him. > **""When he reaches forty in Islam Allah secures him from madness, leprosy, and vitiligo; at fifty his reckoning is lightened; at sixty he is granted repentance; at seventy heaven loves him; at eighty his evil is overlooked; at ninety he is forgiven and intercedes; and in extreme old age his former good is still recorded.""**"
"17556","In one narration from Anas, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that no Muslim granted long life in Islam — then like the preceding — and that when he reaches seventy years in Islam, Allah loves him and the inhabitants of heaven love him. > **""When he reaches seventy years in Islam, Allah loves him and the inhabitants of heaven love him.""**"
"17557","In one narration, when he reaches seventy years in Islam, the inhabitants of heaven and earth love him. > **""When he reaches seventy years in Islam, the inhabitants of heaven and earth love him.""**"
"17558","In one narration, when he reaches sixty Allah grants him turning to Allah in what Allah loves, and when he reaches seventy Allah forgives his past and future sins, he becomes Allah's captive on earth, and he is granted intercession for his household. > **""At sixty Allah grants him turning in what He loves; at seventy He forgives his past and future sins, he becomes Allah's captive on earth, and intercedes for his household.""**"
"17559","Ahmad narrated it briefly as mawquf, with the wording that when he reaches sixty Allah grants him a turning to Him for which He loves him. > **""When he reaches sixty, Allah grants him a turning to Him for which He loves him.""**"
"17560","He then narrated with his chain to ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with them, from the Prophet ﷺ the like thereof. > **""Like the preceding narration on the stages of long Muslim life, through Ibn ʿUmar.""**"
"17561","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that whoever Allah grants forty years in Islam, Allah diverts from him kinds of afflictions — leprosy and vitiligo, and in ʿAbd al-Malik's wording Allah restrains kinds of affliction including leprosy, vitiligo, and Satan's rage — and whoever Allah grants fifty years in Islam, Allah eases his reckoning. > **""Whoever Allah grants forty years in Islam, Allah diverts afflictions from him; and whoever He grants fifty years in Islam, Allah eases his reckoning.""**"
"17562","In one narration, Allah eases his reckoning on the Day of Resurrection; whoever Allah grants sixty years in Islam, Allah grants him turning to Him in what Allah loves — in Abu Damrah's wording, Allah grants him fine turning to Him — and whoever He grants seventy years in Islam, heaven and earth love him; whoever He grants eighty years in Islam, Allah effaces his evil and records his good — in Anas's wording, Allah records his good and does not record his evil — and whoever He grants ninety years in Islam, Allah forgives his sins, he becomes Allah's captive on earth and an intercessor for his household on the Day of Resurrection — in Anas ibn ʿIyad's wording, he is granted intercession for his household. > **""At sixty Allah grants him turning in what He loves; at seventy heaven and earth love him; at eighty his evil is effaced and his good recorded; at ninety he is forgiven, Allah's captive on earth, interceding for his household.""**"
"17563","ʿUthman — meaning Ibn ʿAffan, may Allah be pleased with him — reported from the Prophet ﷺ that when the Muslim servant reaches fifty, Allah lightens his reckoning, and when he reaches sixty Allah grants him turning to Him, and when he reaches seventy the inhabitants of heaven love him, and when he reaches eighty Allah confirms his good and effaces his evil, and when he reaches ninety Allah forgives his past and future sins, grants him intercession for his household, and it is written in heaven that he is Allah's captive on earth. > **""At fifty his reckoning is lightened; at sixty he is granted repentance; at seventy heaven loves him; at eighty his good is confirmed and his evil effaced; at ninety he is forgiven, interceding for his household, Allah's captive on earth.""**"
"17564","ʿAbdullah ibn Abi Bakr al-Siddiq reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when the Muslim reaches fifty, Allah diverts three kinds of affliction — madness, leprosy, and vitiligo; when he reaches sixty Allah grants him turning to Him; when he reaches seventy his evil is effaced and his good recorded; when he reaches ninety Allah forgives his past and future sins, he becomes Allah's captive on earth and an intercessor for his household. > **""At fifty three afflictions are diverted; at sixty he is granted repentance; at seventy his evil is effaced and his good recorded; at ninety he is forgiven, Allah's captive on earth, interceding for his household.""**"
"17565","Sahl ibn Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when a servant reaches sixty, Allah has excused him regarding age and granted him full age. > **""When a servant reaches sixty, Allah has excused him regarding age and granted him full age.""**"
"17566","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the ages of his ummah are between sixty and seventy, and few among them reach eighty. > **""The ages of my ummah are between sixty and seventy, and few among them reach eighty.""**"
"17567","Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, said he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the ages of his ummah, and he said between fifty and sixty; they asked about those of seventy, and he said few of his ummah reach it; may Allah have mercy on those of seventy, and may Allah have mercy on those of eighty. > **""The ages of my ummah are between fifty and sixty; few reach seventy; may Allah have mercy on those of seventy and those of eighty.""**"
"17568","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that few of his ummah are those who reach seventy. > **""Few of my ummah are those who reach seventy.""**"
"17569","ʿAmr ibn ʿAbasah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that none should wish for death unless he trusts in his deeds; but if you see six traits in Islam, then wish for death, and if your soul is in your hand then release it: squandering of blood, rule of children, multitude of police, rule of fools, sale of judgment, and youths who take the Qur'an as flutes. > **""None should wish for death unless he trusts in his deeds; but if you see squandered blood, rule of children, many police, rule of fools, sold judgments, and youths taking the Qur'an as flutes — then wish for death.""**"
"17570","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that none should wish for death nor supplicate for it before it comes, unless he has trusted in his deeds. > **""None should wish for death nor supplicate for it before it comes, unless he has trusted in his deeds.""**"
"17571","Abu al-Muʿalla reported that al-Hakam al-Ghifari, may Allah be pleased with him, said, O plague, take me to you, whereupon a man said, why do you say this when you heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that none should wish for death? He replied that he heard what they heard, but he hastens before six: sale of judgment, multitude of police, rule of children, shedding of blood, severing of kinship, and youths in the last age who take the Qur'an as flutes. > **""I hasten before six: sold judgments, many police, rule of children, shed blood, severed kinship, and youths who take the Qur'an as flutes.""**"
"17572","Yusuf ibn ʿAbdillah ibn Salam, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that he visited Abu al-Darda', may Allah be pleased with him, in the illness in which he died, and he asked what brought him to this land; he replied only the bond between him and his father ʿAbdullah ibn Salam, so Abu al-Darda' said this was a poor hour for falsehood, and that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that whoever performs ablution well, then stands to pray two or four rakʿahs — Sahl doubted — perfecting bowing and humility therein, then seeks Allah's forgiveness, is forgiven. > **""Whoever performs ablution well, prays two or four rakʿahs with bowing and humility, then seeks Allah's forgiveness, is forgiven.""**"
"17573","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that Iblis said to his Lord that by His might and majesty he would never cease misleading the children of Adam while souls remained in them, so his Lord said that by His might and majesty He would never cease forgiving them while they sought His forgiveness. > **""By Your might and majesty I shall never cease misleading them while souls remain in them. By My might and majesty I shall never cease forgiving them while they seek My forgiveness.""**"
"17574","Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ to hold to none is worthy of worship but Allah and seeking forgiveness, for Iblis said he ruined people with sins while they ruined him with none is worthy of worship but Allah and seeking forgiveness, so when he saw that he ruined them with desires while they deemed themselves guided. > **""Hold to none is worthy of worship but Allah and seeking forgiveness; Iblis ruins with sins, is ruined by unity and seeking forgiveness, then ruins with desires while they deem themselves guided.""**"
"17575","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that hearts rust like iron rusts, and their polish is seeking forgiveness. > **""Hearts rust like iron rusts, and their polish is seeking forgiveness.""**"
"17576","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that the companion of the left raises the pen six hours from the erring or offending Muslim servant; if he regrets and seeks Allah's forgiveness for it, it is discarded, otherwise one is recorded. > **""The companion of the left raises the pen six hours from the erring Muslim servant; if he regrets and seeks forgiveness it is discarded, otherwise one is recorded.""**"
"17577","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the companion of the right is trusted over the companion of the left: when the servant does a good deed he records it tenfold, and when he does an evil deed and the companion of the left wishes to record it, the companion of the right tells him to hold back, so he holds back; if he seeks forgiveness it is not recorded, and if he remains silent it is recorded against him. > **""The companion of the right is trusted over the companion of the left; when evil is done he says, hold back — if he seeks forgiveness it is not recorded, otherwise it is recorded.""**"
"17578","Umm ʿIsmah al-ʿAwsiyyah, a woman of Qays, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that no servant commits a sin but the angel charged with counting his sins pauses three hours; if he seeks Allah's forgiveness for that sin within those hours, it will not be held against him on the Day of Resurrection. > **""No servant commits a sin but the angel pauses three hours; if he seeks forgiveness within them, it will not be held against him on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17579","al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever wishes his record to gladden him should increase seeking forgiveness therein. > **""Whoever wishes his record to gladden him should increase seeking forgiveness therein.""**"
"17580","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that no two guardian angels raise to Allah what they guarded in a day, with Allah seeing seeking forgiveness at the start of the record and at its end, but Allah, Blessed and Exalted, says He has forgiven His servant what lies between the two ends of the record. > **""No two angels raise a day's record with seeking forgiveness at its start and end but Allah says: I have forgiven My servant what lies between them.""**"
"17581","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that he seeks Allah's forgiveness seventy times in a day. > **""I seek Allah's forgiveness seventy times in a day.""**"
"17582","In one narration, the Prophet ﷺ said, I repent, in place of, I seek forgiveness. > **""I repent to Allah in a day.""**"
"17583","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that he seeks Allah's forgiveness and repents to Him seventy times. > **""I seek Allah's forgiveness and repent to Him seventy times.""**"
"17584","In one narration, more than seventy times. > **""I seek Allah's forgiveness and repent more than seventy times.""**"
"17585","In one narration, one hundred times. > **""I seek Allah's forgiveness and repent one hundred times.""**"
"17586","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said he was sharp-tongued, most of it against his family, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, where are you from seeking forgiveness? He himself seeks Allah's forgiveness one hundred times in a day and night. > **""Where are you from seeking forgiveness? I seek Allah's forgiveness one hundred times in a day and night.""**"
"17587","Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that he seeks Allah's forgiveness and repents to Him one hundred times in a day. > **""I seek Allah's forgiveness and repent to Him one hundred times in a day.""**"
"17588","ʿAbd al-Rahman ibn Dalham reported that a man said, O Messenger of Allah, teach me a deed by which I enter Paradise, and he said not to become angry and Paradise is his; he asked for more, so he told him to seek Allah's forgiveness seventy times in a day before the sun sets, and Allah will forgive him the sin of seventy years; he said he had no seventy years, so he said then for his father; he said his father had no seventy years, so he said then for his household; he said his household had no seventy years, so he said then for his neighbours. > **""Seek Allah's forgiveness seventy times before sunset and Allah will forgive you seventy years — or your father, your household, or your neighbours.""**"
"17589","ʿUthman ibn Abi al-ʿAs al-Thaqafi, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the gates of heaven open at midnight, and a caller calls whether there is any supplicant to be answered, any asker to be given, any distressed to be relieved, and no Muslim supplicates with a supplication but Allah answers him — except an adulteress who traffics with her private part, or a tax-collector. > **""The gates of heaven open at midnight: is there any supplicant to be answered, asker to be given, distressed to be relieved? Every Muslim is answered except an adulteress or a tax-collector.""**"
"17590","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded them to seek forgiveness seventy times in the pre-dawn hours. > **""Seek forgiveness seventy times in the pre-dawn hours.""**"
"17591","Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say, O Allah, I seek Your forgiveness for what I sent ahead and delayed, kept secret and declared; You are the Advancer and the Delayer, and You are over all things Powerful. > **""O Allah, I seek Your forgiveness for what I sent ahead and delayed, kept secret and declared; You are the Advancer and the Delayer, over all things Powerful.""**"
"17592","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them, raising the narration, said that whoever says glory be to Allah and with His praise, I seek Allah's forgiveness and repent to Him, it is written as he said it, then hung upon the Throne, no sin of its speaker effacing it, until he meets Allah with it sealed as he said it. > **""Whoever says: Glory be to Allah and with His praise, I seek Allah's forgiveness and repent to Him — it is written, hung upon the Throne, sealed until he meets Allah.""**"
"17593","Abu Malik al-Ashʿari, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the most fulfilling word with Allah is for the servant to say, O Allah, You are my Lord and I am Your servant, I wronged myself and confessed my sin, and none forgives sins but You, so O my Lord forgive my sin. > **""O Allah, You are my Lord and I am Your servant, I wronged myself and confessed my sin, and none forgives sins but You, so forgive my sin.""**"
"17594","ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, said that no man says, I seek Allah's forgiveness, besides Whom none is worthy of worship, the Living, the Sustainer, and I repent to Him, three times, but he is forgiven, even if he fled from battle. > **""No man says: I seek Allah's forgiveness besides Whom none is worthy of worship, the Living, the Sustainer, and I repent to Him — three times — but he is forgiven, even if he fled from battle.""**"
"17595","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah raises a rank for the righteous servant in Paradise, and he asks, O my Lord, how is this mine? So He says, through your child's seeking forgiveness for you. > **""Allah raises a rank for the righteous servant in Paradise, and he asks how; He says: Through your child's seeking forgiveness for you.""**"
"17596","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that mountains of good deeds follow a man on the Day of Resurrection, and he asks how this is, so it is said, through your child's seeking forgiveness for you. > **""Mountains of good follow a man and he asks how; it is said: Through your child's seeking forgiveness for you.""**"
"17597","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever has no wealth to give in charity should seek forgiveness for the believing men and women, for it is charity. > **""Whoever has no wealth to give in charity should seek forgiveness for the believing men and women, for it is charity.""**"
"17598","ʿUbadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that whoever seeks forgiveness for the believing men and women, Allah records for him a good deed for every believing man and woman. > **""Whoever seeks forgiveness for the believing men and women, Allah records for him a good deed for every believing man and woman.""**"
"17599","Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever says each day, O Allah forgive me and the believing men and women, is gifted a good deed from every believer. > **""Whoever says each day: O Allah forgive me and the believing men and women — is gifted a good deed from every believer.""**"
"17600","Abu al-Darda', may Allah be pleased with him, heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that whoever seeks forgiveness for the believing men and women each day twenty-seven times — or twenty-five times, one of the two numbers — shall be among those whose prayers are answered and through whom the people of earth are sustained. > **""Whoever seeks forgiveness for the believing men and women each day twenty-seven times shall be among the answered, through whom earth is sustained.""**"
"17601","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that they used to withhold seeking forgiveness for major sinners until they heard their Prophet ﷺ say that Allah does not forgive shirk but forgives less than that for whom He wills, and say that he stored his intercession for the major sinners on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Allah does not forgive shirk but forgives less than that for whom He wills; and I stored my intercession for the major sinners on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17602","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whatever reward Allah promises for a deed, He will fulfil it, but whatever punishment Allah promises for a deed, He may waive it. > **""Whatever reward Allah promises for a deed He will fulfil; whatever punishment He promises for a deed He may waive.""**"
"17603","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever commits a sin knowing that Allah, if He wills, punishes him, and if He wills forgives him, it becomes binding upon Allah to forgive him. > **""Whoever commits a sin knowing that Allah may punish or forgive him, it becomes binding upon Allah to forgive him.""**"
"17604","ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever commits a sin knowing that Allah observed him is forgiven, even if he did not seek forgiveness. > **""Whoever commits a sin knowing that Allah observed him is forgiven, even if he did not seek forgiveness.""**"
"17605","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out one day and said that no sin He forgives is too great for Allah, Exalted in remembrance: a man among those before them killed ninety-eight souls, came to a monk asking if there was repentance for him, and was told he had transgressed excessively, so he rose and killed him; then he came to another monk confessing ninety-nine killings, was told no, he had transgressed, so he killed him; then he came to another confessing one hundred killings, was told he had transgressed but he did not know, yet there were two villages — Nasrah whose people do the deeds of Paradise admitting none else, and Kafrah whose people do the deeds of Hell admitting none else — so he should go to Nasrah, and if he remained there doing their deeds, he should not doubt his repentance; he set out until death seized him between the two villages, the angels asked their Lord, and He told them to see which village he was nearer and record him among its people, and they found him nearer to Nasrah by a fingertip's span, so he was recorded among its people. > **""No sin He forgives is too great for Allah; go to the people of Nasrah, remain among them doing their deeds, and do not doubt your repentance.""**"
"17606","Abu ʿAbd Rabb reported that he heard Muʿawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan, may Allah be pleased with them, upon the minbar narrate that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that a man who wronged himself met another and confessed killing ninety-nine souls unjustly, asking if there was repentance, and was told no, so he killed him; then he met another confessing one hundred unjust killings, and was told that to say Allah does not turn to one who repents would be a lie, but there were a people worshipping, so he should join them in worshipping Allah; he set out, died upon that, the angels of mercy and punishment gathered, Allah sent an angel telling them to measure between the two places and assign him to whichever he was nearer, and they found him nearer to the monastery of the repentant by a fingertip, so he was forgiven. > **""To say Allah does not turn to one who repents would be a lie; join the worshippers — and he was found nearer to the monastery of the repentant by a fingertip, so he was forgiven.""**"
"17607","Abu Qays, freedman of Banu Jumah, reported that he heard Abu Zamʿah al-Balawi — among the companions of the Tree who pledged to the Prophet ﷺ beneath it — stand in the courtyard of the Fustat mosque, having heard of some strictness from ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr, and say not to be strict with people, for he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that a man of the Children of Israel killed ninety-seven souls, went to a monk asking for repentance and was refused, so he killed him; then he killed a second monk after confessing ninety-eight; then he confessed ninety-nine including two monks to a third, who said he had done evil, but to say Allah is not Forgiving, Merciful would be a lie, so he should repent; the man refused to part from him, served him without disobeying, wept intensely at the burial of an ill-praised man upon seeing his evil recompense and laughed intensely at the burial of a well-praised man upon seeing his good recompense, was questioned by his companions, was tested by command to kindle an oven and cast himself therein, obeyed while the monk heedlessly turned away, was found alive sweating in the oven, was taken out by the hand, and thereafter became among the great men of the Children of Israel. > **""To say Allah is not Forgiving, Merciful would be a lie, so repent to Allah — and thereafter he became among the great men of the Children of Israel.""**"
"17608","Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that there were two villages, one righteous and one unjust, and a man left the unjust village intending the righteous one, death seized him where Allah willed, the angel and Satan disputed over him — Satan swearing he never disobeyed him, the angel saying he left intending repentance — so it was decreed to see which he was nearer, and they found him nearer to the righteous village by a span, so he was forgiven; and Maʿmar added that he heard it said Allah drew the righteous village near to him. > **""He left intending repentance and was found nearer to the righteous village by a span, so he was forgiven.""**"
"17609","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ passed with some companions while a child was on the road, and when his mother saw the company she feared he would be trampled, so she rushed crying, my son, my son, and seized him, whereupon the people said she would never cast her son into the Fire, so the Prophet ﷺ calmed them and said that neither does Allah cast His beloved into the Fire. > **""She would never cast her son into the Fire — and neither does Allah cast His beloved into the Fire.""**"
"17610","ʿAbdullah ibn Abi Awfa, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out one day to find a weeping lost child, told ʿUmar to gather him, then his mother came, took him, clasped and kissed him while weeping, so the Prophet ﷺ asked whether they deemed her merciful to her child, and they said yes, whereupon he said that by Allah, Allah is more merciful to the Muslims than she is to her child. > **""Do you deem her merciful to her child? By Allah, Allah is more merciful to the Muslims than she is to her child.""**"
"17611","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah, Blessed and Exalted, created nothing but He created what overcomes it, and He created His mercy to overcome His anger. > **""Allah created nothing but He created what overcomes it, and He created His mercy to overcome His anger.""**"
"17612","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he asked Jibril whether his Lord — Exalted in remembrance, Sublime in grandeur — prays, and he said yes; he asked what His prayer is, and he said: Glory, Holiness — My mercy preceded My anger. > **""Glory, Holiness — My mercy preceded My anger.""**"
"17613","Abu Saʿid, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that if they knew the measure of Allah's mercy, they would rely upon it. > **""If you knew the measure of Allah's mercy, you would rely upon it.""**"
"17614","Jundub, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a Bedouin alighted, hobbled his mount, and after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed, he untied it, mounted, and called, O Allah, have mercy on me and Muhammad, associating none in our mercy, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, do you say he is more astray or his camel? Did you not hear what he said? They said yes, so he said he had confined the vast mercy of Allah, for Allah created one hundred mercies, sent down one by which creatures — jinn, men, and beasts — show mutual kindness, keeping ninety-nine with Him; do you say he is more astray or his camel? > **""You confined the vast mercy of Allah: He created one hundred mercies, sent down one by which creatures show kindness, keeping ninety-nine with Him.""**"
"17615","al-Hasan al-Basri conveyed that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah has one hundred mercies, one of which He divided among the people of earth so it sufficed them until their terms, storing ninety-nine with Him for His saints, and on the Day of Resurrection He will reclaim the earthly mercy to complete one hundred for His saints. > **""Allah divided one mercy among earth so it sufficed them, storing ninety-nine for His saints, completing one hundred for them on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17616","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ the like thereof concerning the one hundred mercies. > **""Like the preceding narration on the one hundred mercies, through Abu Hurayrah.""**"
"17617","Khilas reported the like thereof concerning the one hundred mercies. > **""Like the preceding narration on the one hundred mercies, through Khilas.""**"
"17618","Muhammad ibn Sirin reported the like thereof concerning the one hundred mercies. > **""Like the preceding narration on the one hundred mercies, through Muhammad ibn Sirin.""**"
"17619","Muʿawiyah ibn Haydah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that Allah, Exalted and Mighty, created one hundred mercies, one mercy among His creation by which they show mutual mercy, storing ninety-nine for His saints. > **""Allah created one hundred mercies: one among creation by which they show mercy, storing ninety-nine for His saints.""**"
"17620","ʿUbadah — meaning Ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him — reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that their Lord divided His mercy into one hundred parts, sending down one part to earth by which people, birds, and beasts show mutual mercy, while one hundred mercies less one part remain with Him for His servants on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Our Lord divided mercy into one hundred parts: one upon earth by which creatures show mercy, the rest with Him for His servants on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17621","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah, Exalted and Mighty, created one hundred mercies, one of which He divided among creatures, and ninety-nine until the Day of Resurrection. > **""Allah created one hundred mercies: one divided among creatures, and ninety-nine until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17622","al-Farazdaq ibn Ghalib reported that he met Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, in Sham, who asked if he was the poet, and he said yes, so he warned that if he lived he would meet people saying there was no repentance for him, so he should never cut off his hope from Allah's mercy. > **""If you meet people saying there is no repentance for you, never cut off your hope from Allah's mercy.""**"
"17623","Thawban, freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he would not love to have the world and all therein in exchange for this verse: O My servants who transgressed against themselves, do not despair of Allah's mercy. > **""I would not love the world and all therein in exchange for: O My servants who transgressed against themselves, do not despair of Allah's mercy.""**"
"17624","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent to Wahshi, the killer of Hamzah, inviting him to Islam, but he replied asking how he invites him while claiming that whoever kills, associates partners, or commits adultery meets retribution multiplied on the Day of Resurrection abiding humiliated, when he had done all that — was there any concession? So Allah revealed except whoever repents, believes, and does righteous deeds, for those Allah replaces their evil with good, and Allah is Forgiving, Merciful; Wahshi replied that this was a severe condition of repentance, belief, and righteous deeds which he might not fulfil, so Allah revealed that Allah does not forgive shirk but forgives less than that for whom He wills; he replied that this left the matter to will and he did not know if he was intended, so Allah revealed O My servants who transgressed against themselves, do not despair of Allah's mercy, indeed Allah forgives all sins, whereupon he accepted and embraced Islam. > **""O My servants who transgressed against themselves, do not despair of Allah's mercy; indeed Allah forgives all sins — whereupon he accepted and embraced Islam.""**"
"17625","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the expiation of sin is remorse, and that had they not sinned Allah would have brought a people who sin in order to forgive them. > **""The expiation of sin is remorse; had you not sinned, Allah would have brought a people who sin in order to forgive them.""**"
"17626","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that had they not sinned, Allah would have created a creation who sin, then He forgives them — and in the Awsat wording, who sin then seek Allah's forgiveness, so He forgives them, and He is the Forgiving, the Merciful. > **""Had you not sinned, Allah would have created a creation who sin, seek forgiveness, and He forgives them, and He is the Forgiving, the Merciful.""**"
"17627","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that had they not sinned, Allah would have removed them and brought a people who sin, then seek Allah's forgiveness, so He forgives them. > **""Had you not sinned, Allah would have removed you and brought a people who sin, seek forgiveness, and He forgives them.""**"
"17628","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says, O son of Adam, so long as you supplicate Me and hope in Me, I forgive you for whatever came from you; were you to come to Me with the earth full of faults, I would meet you with the earth full of forgiveness, so long as you associate none with Me; and were your faults to reach the clouds of the sky, then you sought My forgiveness, I would forgive you. > **""O son of Adam, so long as you supplicate Me and hope in Me I forgive you; were you to come with the earth full of sins without shirk, I would meet you with its full of forgiveness; were your sins to reach the sky then you sought Me, I would forgive you.""**"
"17629","Abu al-Darda', may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that their Lord, Blessed and Exalted, says that were His servant to meet Him with the earth full of sins associating nothing with Him, He would meet him with its full of forgiveness. > **""Were My servant to meet Me with the earth full of sins associating nothing with Me, I would meet him with its full of forgiveness.""**"
"17630","Abu al-Darda', may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ, from Jibril, peace be upon him, from his Lord, Mighty and Majestic, that He says, My servant, were you to meet Me with the earth full of sins, I would meet you with the like thereof in forgiveness, and I would not care. > **""My servant, were you to meet Me with the earth full of sins, I would meet you with its like in forgiveness, and I would not care.""**"
"17631","With the same chain, Abu al-Darda', may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ, from Jibril, peace be upon him, from his Lord, Mighty and Majestic, that He says, My servant, so long as you supplicate Me and hope in Me, associating nothing with Me, I forgive you for whatever is in you. > **""My servant, so long as you supplicate Me and hope in Me, associating nothing with Me, I forgive you for whatever is in you.""**"
"17632","Hudhayfah — meaning Ibn al-Yaman, may Allah be pleased with him — reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, by Him in Whose Hand is his soul, Allah shall admit to Paradise the profligate in religion and the feeble-minded in livelihood; by Him in Whose Hand is his soul, Allah shall admit to Paradise one whom the Fire scorched for his sin; and by Him in Whose Hand is his soul, Allah shall forgive on the Day of Resurrection a forgiveness for which Iblis stretches forth hoping it reaches him — with the addition, a forgiveness that never crossed a human heart. > **""By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, Allah shall admit the profligate and the scorched sinner to Paradise, and shall forgive a forgiveness for which even Iblis stretches forth in hope.""**"
"17633","Abu Hurayrah — or Abu Saʿid, al-Aʿmash doubted — reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has freed ones in every day and night, each with an answered supplication. > **""Allah has freed ones in every day and night, each with an answered supplication.""**"
"17634","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has six hundred thousand freed from the Fire on every Friday, all of whom had deserved the Fire. > **""Allah has six hundred thousand freed from the Fire on every Friday, all of whom had deserved the Fire.""**"
"17635","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah has — in an hour of the hours of the world — six hundred thousand freed whom He frees from the Fire, all of whom had deserved the Fire. > **""Allah has, in an hour of the world, six hundred thousand freed from the Fire, all of whom had deserved it.""**"
"17636","Abu al-Darda', may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to revere Allah and He will forgive them; Ibn Thawban explained it to mean to embrace Islam. > **""Revere Allah and He will forgive you — meaning, embrace Islam.""**"
"17637","Abu al-Darda', may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that were what they do to animals forgiven them, much else would be forgiven them. > **""Were what you do to animals forgiven you, much else would be forgiven you.""**"
"17638","al-Harith ibn Suwayd reported that he heard ʿAbdullah — meaning Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him — say that there is no word he speaks before a ruler warding off two lashes of the whip from himself but he would speak it. > **""There is no word I speak before a ruler warding off two lashes but I would speak it.""**"
"17639","Muʿadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that no soul dies with an atom's weight of good with Allah but clay is sealed over it. > **""No soul dies with an atom's weight of good with Allah but clay is sealed over it.""**"
"17640","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the Lord, Mighty and Majestic, says the servant's good and evil deeds are brought on the Day of Resurrection and set against one another, and if a single good deed remains, Allah admits him to Paradise; he asked, what if no good remains? He said, those are the ones from whom We accept the best of what they did and overlook their evil among the companions of Paradise; he asked about the verse no soul knows what coolness of eyes is hidden for them, and he said it is the servant who does a secret deed which Allah kept hidden for him on the Day of Resurrection, where he sees coolness of eyes. > **""The servant's good and evil are set against one another; if a single good remains, Allah admits him to Paradise; and the secret deed is kept hidden until he sees coolness of eyes.""**"
